<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manuel Belgrano</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1770">1770</a>–<a href="/years/1820">1820</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Manuel Belgrano was an Argentine economist, politician and general of the wars of independence. A member of the first junta of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> in 1810, he led an expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> that was defeated in 1811. He created the blue and white flag of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> in 1812 and won the battles of Tucumán and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salta">Salta</a> against royalist armies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manuel-belgrano.jpg" alt="Manuel Belgrano" width="320" height="388" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Casimir Carbonnier · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manuel_belgrano_by_carbonier_retouched.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Manuel Belgrano was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> on 3 June 1770, the fourth child of the Italian merchant Domingo Belgrano y Peri. As a criollo he ranked below the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peninsulares">peninsulares</a>. He studied at the San Carlos school, then in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, where he chose law rather than commerce. A papal dispensation let him read forbidden authors such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Rousseau</a>. He returned in 1794 as perpetual secretary of the Commerce Consulate of Buenos Aires and held the post until 1810. Unable to change the economic system, he put his effort into education, advocating schools of agriculture and commerce and free primary schools for poor children.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Belgrano first took part in fighting during the first British invasion of the River Plate, when his untrained men scattered after a single <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> shot. After Ferdinand VII was deposed, he led the Carlotist movement, which sought to put the king's sister Carlota Joaquina, then in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a>, in his place.</p><p>He took part in the plot against the viceroy Baltasar Hidalgo de Cisneros in May 1810 and was elected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primera-junta">Primera Junta</a>. As the junta's delegate he led the campaign to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a>, where Bernardo de Velasco beat him at Paraguarí and Tacuarí.</p><p>He designed the flag while fortifying Rosario. The First Triumvirate did not approve it, and Belgrano learned this only weeks later, at Jujuy. Facing royalist armies from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-peru">Upper Peru</a>, he ordered the Jujuy Exodus, which moved the province's whole population to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-miguel-de-tucuman">San Miguel de Tucumán</a>. His counter-offensive won the Battle of Tucumán and then a full victory over Pío Tristán at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salta">Salta</a>.</p><p>Defeats at Vilcapugio and Ayohuma ended his incursion into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-peru">Upper Peru</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-san-martin">José de San Martín</a> replaced him. Belgrano then went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernardino-rivadavia">Bernardino Rivadavia</a> to seek support, and on his return took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-tucuman">Congress of Tucumán</a>, which declared independence in 1816. He backed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> plan for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> under an Inca descendant, which delegates from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> strongly rejected.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Belgrano took command of the Army of the North again, but his troops mutinied in January 1820. Gravely ill, he was briefly imprisoned in Tucumán during a coup and was released when it ended. He died of dropsy on 20 June 1820, reportedly saying, Oh, my homeland. His flag was approved by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-tucuman">Congress of Tucumán</a> as the national flag. Argentines regard him as one of the main Founding Fathers, and National Flag Day is held on 20 June in memory of his death.</p><p>A bronze equestrian statue stands in the Plaza de Mayo, and a mausoleum by Ettore Ximenes, completed in 1903, holds his ashes at the Santo Domingo Convent. He remained a monarchist, favouring a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> on the British pattern, and he translated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a>'s Farewell Address into Spanish.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Manuel Belgrano” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Buenos Aires sent an army under <b>Manuel Belgrano</b>, but Paraguayan forces defeated it at Paraguarí on 19 January and at Tacuarí on 9 March 1811.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-paraguay">Republic of Paraguay · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Manuel Belgrano</b> raised the new light blue and white flag at Rosario in 1812 without asking; the First Triumvirate ordered him to hide it and sent him a Spanish red and yellow one, but he had already left for the north and never received the message.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-provinces-of-the-rio-de-la-plata">United Provinces of the Río de la Plata · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1811 it rejected the authority of Buenos Aires, defeated <b>Manuel Belgrano's</b> army, and in May overthrew the governor, becoming the independent Republic of Paraguay.</blockquote><a href="/state/governorate-of-paraguay">Governorate of Paraguay</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/argentina">Argentina</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/argentina">Argentina</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-34.6,-58.38&amp;year=1802" aria-label="Buenos Aires on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3067 3416 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Manuel Belgrano"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3517 3686h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3517 3686h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-34.6,-58.38&amp;year=1802">What was here in 1802? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
